#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23c4516d-ba9c-5b55-b971-ceeb575d50c3" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e583ae8-d9fe-52a9-b929-554bdbe54145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559091} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2007b5-5f0d-5777-a12b-059ac6926d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559133} {:answer/id #uuid "9f06c2d4-90c4-5f39-a2ec-6d108a9826a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559419} {:answer/id #uuid "a94df82a-733b-5f27-86b0-983a28681f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559441} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb940e-d44e-5a8f-988a-50c419452c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb940e-d44e-5a8f-988a-50c419452c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559584} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4b18df5-7e14-5ba9-ad19-fa824c06c4c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c04c2ae6-0944-57e5-bf4e-7441e7fccca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-112376" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+x-6?$\n\nI. $x=3$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=3$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ={3}^{2}+(3)-6 \\\\  & =9+3-6 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=3$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x-3)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x-3)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd5d3a7d-ef95-5d7d-a92a-ce76d6b400bb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem to Factors With Leading Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50a01771-cde1-54b6-903b-5be07bb31b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559222} {:answer/id #uuid "7215867e-586e-537b-aa3c-c7a87d387e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559298} {:answer/id #uuid "7b90dcb3-20f2-5889-885c-583181c9f4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559323} {:answer/id #uuid "a0014fe0-63da-51e9-bf6d-ad45b394652b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559421} {:answer/id #uuid "fedcbdd6-f4a6-5750-a08c-d1f7b5c28221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7215867e-586e-537b-aa3c-c7a87d387e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559298} :answer-field/id #uuid "8508e80e-2237-5e61-952e-da76d1a6dd3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cef78ed2-9af9-556a-8a9d-05a7101331ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-112468" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-3x+2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(2x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(2x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(2x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{1}{2}) & ={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}-3(-\\frac{1}{2})+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23c4516d-ba9c-5b55-b971-ceeb575d50c3" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e82de2-f5a4-5a17-8312-4f73519d2e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559150} {:answer/id #uuid "47ca42e7-e9b5-518e-9713-80c7cee79696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559203} {:answer/id #uuid "9e82d34b-fe77-5fe5-9e29-97290fc018c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559418} {:answer/id #uuid "c527e597-42e6-5d49-9d63-cfb389529772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559499} {:answer/id #uuid "fec4a528-b0f6-5c07-bfc6-11532012aef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35e82de2-f5a4-5a17-8312-4f73519d2e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559150} :answer-field/id #uuid "f84d9338-e966-5479-8801-6e8f15658f84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81cebe4e-1d65-5357-b789-c0fc16951ab5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38040" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x+3)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-8x+3$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-x-3$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x-2$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=-3$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-3$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =2(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-8(-3)+3 \\\\  & =2(-27)+3(9)+24+3 \\\\  & =-54+27+24+3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-3$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-(-3)-3 \\\\  & =-27+3(9)+3-3 \\\\  & =-27+27+3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-3$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+2(-3)^{2}-(-3)-2 \\\\  & =-27+2(9)+3-2 \\\\  & =-27+18+3-2 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23c4516d-ba9c-5b55-b971-ceeb575d50c3" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2c79867-73d6-5cc0-a3af-25cf7ccb5cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559495} {:answer/id #uuid "c845ff5b-3e9e-5056-902c-a2214e1aa90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559503} {:answer/id #uuid "d069d041-5456-57d8-b6c8-da7c7f8df610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559520} {:answer/id #uuid "f02ef9f3-2866-5904-be26-7b7de039fb1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559599} {:answer/id #uuid "fce13d87-4fc4-577a-a664-6cc3884100f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f02ef9f3-2866-5904-be26-7b7de039fb1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559599} :answer-field/id #uuid "c70a0b59-1c4a-5aa7-bb33-d1a6a216cf95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e986899c-1ec0-569d-94ba-d6811b53879c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73779" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x-2)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{3}-4x$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}+4x+5$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{2}-4x+4$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=2$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=2$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=2$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & ={2}^{3}-4(2) \\\\  & =8-8 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=2$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={2}^{2}+4(2)+5 \\\\  & =4+8+5 \\\\  & =17\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=2$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(2) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+4 \\\\  & =4-8+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only .\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd5d3a7d-ef95-5d7d-a92a-ce76d6b400bb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem to Factors With Leading Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016050f4-b327-51eb-ac59-6cdb4f2d394b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559017} {:answer/id #uuid "1649ec6b-8d70-5b6e-8662-99e140405802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559064} {:answer/id #uuid "80f25a70-a063-50e7-b4ec-df4df125ef08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559338} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a088d6-83f2-526a-b6ad-dd5f6c749f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559492} {:answer/id #uuid "cc39eb67-2c40-56a3-bfba-b0087f7d23c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80f25a70-a063-50e7-b4ec-df4df125ef08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559338} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e9c46f9-73fa-5bae-8ba5-d402d0aa2e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50aaea5b-0826-518a-8a27-2b0395848ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73789" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-3x-2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(3x+2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+2{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-3(-\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =3(-\\frac{8}{27})+2(\\frac{4}{9})+3(\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{9}+\\frac{8}{9}+2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
